5BSD Ports
==========
Canonical source: https://github.com/5BSD/5BSD-ports.git
Install the source tree at /usr/ports. Build native packages against the same
5BSD base as the destination system. Do not disable the pkg OSVERSION check
to install packages built for a newer upstream base.

Binary packages and Git sources are different services. The base repository
configuration uses 5BSD-ports at file:///usr/5bsd-packages/ports. Publish the
native package catalogue there with pkg repo. Remote deployments must supply
a real package-server URL and signing policy in
/usr/local/etc/pkg/repos/5BSD-ports.conf. No public catalogue is claimed here.
For this host, the custom checkout is /usr/obj/5BSD-ports (selected through
PORTSDIR); /usr/ports remains the conventional checkout location. Base and
matching hardware packages are built separately and published as one verified
repository under /usr/obj/usr/src/repo. Use the maintained system-packages,
hardware-packages, hardware-packages-check and publish-packages source
targets. Base-only output advances latest-built; only complete publication
advances latest. Hardware staging belongs under /usr/obj, not a second
configured hardware update repository or a home-directory repository.


What 5BSD maintains beyond upstream FreeBSD
-----------------------------------------
Inventory checked 2026-10-08 at ports commit a10d155af. This is an inventory
of the checkout, not a claim that every listed project is packaged or tested.
Most of this tree remains upstream FreeBSD ports. Compared with the imported
baseline e3ad8fc58, the custom port origins are:

  lang/dart                    3.13.5  Native Dart SDK and bootstrap support
  x11-toolkits/flutter-engine   0.1.0   Native Flutter GTK engine and tooling
  sysutils/5bsd-instruments     0.3.9   Native Flutter instrumentation app
  sysutils/5bsd-imager          0.2.0   Native Flutter image-writing app

These ports name kory@5bsd.org as maintainer. Dart and the Flutter engine use
the same pinned 5bsd-flutter source snapshot; Dart and Flutter engine ports
are currently amd64-only. Imager is also amd64-only. Imager's application
source lives under sysutils/5bsd-imager/files/app in this checkout. Category
registrations, toolkit smoke tooling and this documentation are also local
maintenance. Package versions here are an audit snapshot; port Makefiles
remain authoritative.

The source/bootstrap archives are referenced through the 5BSD-ports release
area, not an identified separate public Dart/Flutter fork. Record source
revision, downstream patch provenance and bootstrap provenance with releases.
On 2026-10-08 the public API for toolkit-20261004 returned 404; availability
of the release and exact checksummed assets remains a publication blocker.
Existing validation notes below describe prior work, not tests rerun during
this inventory audit. No additional Imager validation is claimed here.

GGML CPU affinity: misc/ggml now carries a native FreeBSD pthread affinity
patch and an explicit pthread link dependency (port revision 2). Vulkan-only
LLVM/SPIR-V build dependencies are conditional, so the CPU-only package builds
with base Clang. Its base-OpenMP model runs pass at 8/16 threads using
KMP_BLOCKTIME=0 on the busy validation host. All 21 GGML CTest cases and
101 explicit CPU ADD cases pass. Requested
CPU masks now take effect; NUMA policy and thread priority are unchanged. Validation
and limitations are recorded in README.mojo-hardware.5BSD. This is a downstream
patch to the upstream project, not a separate 5BSD source fork.

llama.cpp test integration: misc/llama-cpp declares Jinja2 for Python template
comparisons and supplies the selected Python flavor through python3. Its
vocabulary wrapper accepts verified offline fixtures and fails on missing
fixtures or tokenizer errors. All 60 CPU CTest entries pass across the main
run and focused retests. See misc/llama-cpp/files/README.tests.5BSD.

Mojo dependency work (2026-10-09): devel/bazel9 revision 4 builds its native
launcher with the local JDK toolchain and standalone Java compilation; native
version, stage QA and plist checks pass. java/openjdk21 revision 2 preserves
the service-agent's int-sized C boolean when FreeBSD headers include stdbool.h;
its native package now builds and passes virtual-thread/process checks and
Bazel C++/Java/Python build actions. Clean VM qualification continues.
devel/glib20 revision 1 consistently selects base libelf to match its existing
base ELF headers; an installed elfutils pkg-config file can no longer add an
undeclared, ABI-mismatched dependency. ELF resource listing/extraction passes.
lang/python312 is updated to
3.12.15_1 with reviewed POP3/IMAP injection backports, matching doc distfiles
and scoped VuXML corrections. Native mail tests pass (172 pass, four network
skips and two upstream Cyrus-specific skips), as do all 93 Mojo Python interop cases. See
lang/python312/files/README.security.5BSD for provenance and compatibility.

Projects to add or qualify in ports
----------------------------------
Mojo: native CPU development port at lang/mojo.
Hardware/runtime qualification, Pi 4/5 targets and inference boundaries:
README.mojo-hardware.5BSD.
Upstream: https://github.com/modular/modular (Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions).
Downstream FreeBSD/5BSD integration of revision
babedf564bd7e128bd161453f341f6b4b61aa3ca (Mojo 1.2.0.dev2026100805).
The compiler builds with base Clang 21.1.8 and the native Bazel 9.2.0_4 package,
using private pinned LLVM/MLIR 24 sources. No base changes were needed.
On 2026-10-09, the expanded native CPU run finished with 1169 passing
Bazel targets, 6 skipped targets and no failures. Nine more CPU documentation
targets, 12 native LSP cases, all 259 stdlib LSP parse checks and six configuration
cases also pass. The port now includes the native language server and formatter. The formatter
passes 1004 unit cases with two upstream skips; its relocated 67-file package
passes formatting, compile/run and error-handling checks. A staged-package
Python run passes all 93 importer/extension cases. Real operators, process,
life-grid and Python examples run. Stage QA, plist checks and development
package creation pass; a fresh unpack compiles examples and preserves ASan
crash reports with Mojo tracing enabled or disabled. The 503 checksummed
inputs pass ports fetch/checksum/extract/patch validation; dependency analysis
passes with repository downloads disabled. A fresh complete ports build using
a completed Bazel package, clean-VM installation/removal, and full GPU/MAX
suites remain unqualified. This is not a release-qualified package.
Keep Mojo's LLVM/MLIR private; base upgrades are a separate qualification.
See README.mojo.5BSD, README.mojo-tests.5BSD, and
/usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/native-cpu-final/.

BSDGhidra: https://github.com/5BSD/BSDGhidra (default branch master).
This is an actual 5BSD fork, with FreeBSD PTY, build and LLDB integration
recorded in FREEBSD_LLDB_STATUS.md. It is not yet a custom port in this
checkout. Upstream-derived devel/ghidra is not evidence that this fork is
packaged. Decide whether to patch that origin or create a distinct origin;
avoid conflicting installations. Pin the fork revision and offline build
dependencies. Qualify Java/native helpers, headless analysis, GUI, and the
Python-enabled LLDB debugger path. Its llvm19/Python 3.11 examples are not
our package version policy; use one explicitly matched, supported pair.

ESP SDK: planned, interpreted here as Espressif ESP-IDF. A 5BSD-specific
source repository was not found in the public organization inventory;
confirm its source and maintainer before adding a claimed fork URL.
Upstream: https://github.com/espressif/esp-idf
Existing upstream-derived comms/py-esptool covers flashing tooling, not the
whole SDK. Package the supported compiler/toolchain, Python dependencies,
CMake/Ninja, OpenOCD and target support with pinned versions and offline
inputs. Test native 5BSD builds, flashing and debugging on selected Xtensa
and RISC-V ESP boards. Do not describe Linux prebuilt tools as native support.

5BSD project repository directory
--------------------------------
The public organization API listed these repositories on 2026-10-08. This
is a discovery directory, not a promise that each is independently supported
or installable. Confirm integration/ownership before creating new ports for
kernel facilities that may already be part of base.

  https://github.com/5BSD/5BSD
    Operating-system source; base/pkgbase integration, not a ports package.
  https://github.com/5BSD/5BSD-ports
    This custom ports collection and toolkit release-asset location.
  https://github.com/5BSD/BSDGhidra
    Reverse-engineering suite fork; packaging work described above.
  https://github.com/5BSD/FreeBSDKit
    Project repository; standalone packaging/support status not established.
  https://github.com/5BSD/mac_abac
    Attribute-based access-control project; audit base overlap and relevance.
  https://github.com/5BSD/Keyvault
    Kernel key-storage/capability project; audit base overlap.
  https://github.com/5BSD/SecurityCoalitions
    Process/resource coalition project; audit base overlap.
  https://github.com/5BSD/CapabilityAccessControlList
    Capability access-list project; audit base overlap.
  https://github.com/5BSD/OpenEndpointSecurity
    Endpoint-security project; audit base overlap and userland packaging.
  https://github.com/5BSD/ObservableBSD
    Observation/telemetry project; audit current build and packaging needs.

Do not count upstream-derived LLVM, Ghidra, DRM, firmware or esptool ports as
5BSD-maintained forks merely because our images select them. Record actual
patches, origin, upstream revision, maintainer and test coverage when that
changes. Keep a clear distinction between software selection and downstream
source maintenance.

Maintenance requirements
------------------------
For each added project, declare its canonical repository, pinned revision,
license/redistribution terms, supported architectures, native build/runtime
dependencies, package ownership, upgrade behavior and smoke tests. Fetch
all inputs through checksummed ports distfiles; avoid network downloads in
build stages. Submit portable fixes upstream and remove superseded patches.

Review the delta against the FreeBSD import baseline at each sync. Preserve
upstream maintainers on untouched origins. Update this inventory whenever
custom origins are added, removed or upstreamed, and publish reproducible
release archives before advertising successful public fetch/install.

Native toolkit
--------------
lang/dart builds the native 5BSD Dart SDK from source, using a checksummed
native bootstrap SDK. The port is amd64-only until other architectures are
validated. It does not use a Linux runtime. Normal ports fetch/checksum,
configure, build, stage, package, test and plist checks are supported.

Validation performed: source build on 5BSD, stage QA and plist checks;
installation through a 5BSD test catalogue in an isolated 5BSD VM; native
runtime/isolate/file operations and ahead-of-time compilation plus execution.
VM validation also built/packaged print/indexinfo from this tree, checked all
Dart distfiles through ports, and reinstalled Dart from the canonical local
5BSD-ports catalogue using the new base configuration.
This is not a claim of a clean-room VM rebuild of the entire SDK.

Release preparation is incomplete: toolkit source/bootstrap archives are
staged locally, but the proposed toolkit-20261004 GitHub release has not been
published. Public fetch will not work until those exact checksummed archives
are uploaded. Do not publish a different archive under the same filename.
The source exporter records the patched working-tree provenance; this is a
development snapshot, not an assertion of an unmodified upstream checkout.

The companion origins are x11-toolkits/flutter-engine and
sysutils/5bsd-instruments. The engine builds the 5BSD GTK library, matching
gen_snapshot tool, public headers and patched Dart platform SDK from the same
source snapshot as the Dart port. Instruments builds an AOT application offline
from pinned Dart packages and packages its audited DTrace scripts in an
account-independent capability bundle. The port verifies the bundle, tests
the registration lifecycle in a disposable filesystem, and includes package
hooks to refresh an existing registration on upgrade or remove it on uninstall.
Both companion ports pass local package creation, stage QA and packing-list
checks. The packaged GUI passes Wayland and X11 application self-tests with
the staged engine; these tests cover rendering, input, clipboard,
accessibility, native Dart operations, and PF table parsing and sorting.

After installing Instruments, a root administrator must register one desktop
account with that account's actual display session using
5bsd-instruments-register. The helper writes the immutable bundle into the
System capability plane, grants the exact BSDTrace client label if needed,
and reloads switchboard. See the package message for the full command. The
base must include the system.Network.PF endpoint and working BSDTrace; the GUI
package does not replace either system service. The current bundle supports
one registered desktop account per host.

The engine and Instruments source archives are still staged locally pending
publication of the exact checksummed toolkit-20261004 release assets. A fresh
remote ports fetch and a full GUI install on a VM have not yet been validated.

2026-10-10 PF consolidation:
security/sshguard revision 3 selects the PF backend by default and installs
only the PF firewall script (the native hosts helper remains). Remove the
retired IPFW/IPFilter and other-platform scripts from pkg-plist. net/mpd5
revision 20 removes the upstream IPFW configure probe and compile definition.
Both pinned distfiles pass their existing SHA256 checks; port patches apply
and private builds/staging and check-plist pass. No package installation performed. Evidence:
/usr/obj/5BSD-pf-consolidation-20261010/{sshguard,mpd}-stage.log.
